                "item_title": "<small>Lunch break</small>",
                "icon": {
                  "id": 3209,
                  "url": "/uploads/2022/04/icon_lunch.png",
                  "alt": "Lunch icon",
                  "width": 60,
                  "height": 60
                }
              },
              {
                "item_type": "talk",
                "start_time": "14:00:00",
                "end_time": "14:30:00",
                "selected_talk": 3202,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": null,
                "item_title": null,
                "icon": null
              },
              {
                "item_type": "other",
                "start_time": "14:30:00",
                "end_time": null,
                "selected_talk": null,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": null,
                "item_title": "<small>20-minute break</small>",
                "icon": {
                  "id": 3206,
                  "url": "/uploads/2022/04/icon_coffee.png",
                  "alt": "Coffee icon",
                  "width": 55,
                  "height": 54
                }
              },
              {
                "item_type": "talk",
                "start_time": "14:50:00",
                "end_time": "15:20:00",
                "selected_talk": 3213,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": "Some time ago me and my colleague set out to create our own, typescript-focused react form library. It turned out to be one of the most fun experiences of my career, full of interesting engineering challenges. We ended up creating Atom-based state management, composable value decoders, an alternative Promise implementation, and more - just for the purpose of our project. \r\n\r\nThis talk will focus on our journey, what we have learned, and the technical details rather than the library itself.",
                "item_title": null,
                "icon": null
              },
              {
                "item_type": "other",
                "start_time": "15:20:00",
                "end_time": null,
                "selected_talk": null,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": null,
                "item_title": "<small>20-minute break</small>",
                "icon": {
                  "id": 3206,
                  "url": "/uploads/2022/04/icon_coffee.png",
                  "alt": "Coffee icon",
                  "width": 55,
                  "height": 54
                }
              },
              {
                "item_type": "talk",
                "start_time": "15:40:00",
                "end_time": "16:10:00",
                "selected_talk": 3230,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": "Time series are everywhere in science and business, and the ability to forecast them accurately and efficiently can provide decisive advantages. Darts is an open-source Python library that provides a wide variety of forecasting models and tools under a single and user-friendly API. It emphasizes reducing the experiment cycle duration and improving the ease of using, comparing, and combining different models, from ARIMA to deep learning models.\r\nThis talk will give a tour of Darts and some of its main features, such as quick creation and comparison of forecasting models, backtesting, and ML-based models applied to time series forecasting. We will review a few toy examples and see how to address them in a few lines of code.",
                "item_title": null,
                "icon": null
              },
              {
                "item_type": "other",
                "start_time": "16:10:00",
                "end_time": null,
                "selected_talk": null,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": null,
                "item_title": "<small>20-minute break</small>",
                "icon": {
                  "id": 3206,
                  "url": "/uploads/2022/04/icon_coffee.png",
                  "alt": "Coffee icon",
                  "width": 55,
                  "height": 54
                }
              },
              {
                "item_type": "talk",
                "start_time": "16:30:00",
                "end_time": "17:00:00",
                "selected_talk": 3282,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": "Our clients don't know what they need. This happens because no one can foresee the future.\r\nRequirements will evolve and change rapidly over the lifecycle of the project. That's why we need to constantly refine systems.\r\nWe can keep building and discarding prototypes, but it's extremely costly.\r\nOften we can't afford to rewrite everything from scratch only because some new domain insights were discovered.\r\n\r\nThis eventually leads to a mismatched model.\r\nIs there another way?\r\n\r\nWe can focus on domain only and refine our model through examples, until we find the right conceptual contours (http://ddd.fed.wiki.org/view/welcome-visitors/view/conceptual-contours).\r\nEach such iteration of the model is a kind of experiment challenging the upfront decisions.\r\n\r\nDuring this presentation I'm going to show how you can use example mapping combined with business-oriented TDD to be able to run rapid experiments directly on the domain model, without a need to rewrite the system again.",
                "item_title": null,
                "icon": null
              },
              {
                "item_type": "other",
                "start_time": "17:00:00",
                "end_time": "17:15:00",
                "selected_talk": null,
                "is_keynote": false,
                "link_talk": false,
                "talk_summary": null,
                "item_title": "Closing remarks",
                "icon": null
              }
            ],
            "add_load_more_button": false,
            "show_more_index": null
